window.NT_REPORT = [{"id":1,"title":"The Question","paragraphs":["Each Gospel is built from discrete narrative and teaching units — pericopes (healings, parables, controversies, sayings, passion events). When the four Gospels are laid side by side, a striking pattern emerges: three of them (Matthew, Mark, Luke) largely share a common backbone of pericopes in a common order, while the fourth (John) shares very little of either the content or the sequence.","And even among the three \"Synoptic\" Gospels, the shared order breaks down at predictable points. Mapping where the order holds and where it breaks tells us something real about how each author worked and what each was trying to accomplish theologically."]},{"id":2,"title":"Mark as the Shared Backbone","paragraphs":["Of the three Synoptics, Mark supplies the base sequence. Roughly 90% of Mark's pericopes appear in Matthew, and about 50% appear in Luke — and where they appear, they almost always appear in Mark's relative order. This is the single most important structural fact in Gospel studies, and it is the main evidence behind Markan priority (the view, held by most scholars, that Mark was written first and used as a source by Matthew and Luke).","When Matthew or Luke includes a Markan pericope, it is very rare for them to move it far from its Markan position. Where they do move things, it is almost always for a legible reason — which is the interesting part."],"list":["John the Baptist → Jesus' baptism → temptation","Galilean ministry: calling of disciples, exorcisms, healings, early controversies","Parables of the Kingdom","Stilling of the storm → Gerasene demoniac → Jairus' daughter","Rejection at Nazareth","Mission of the Twelve; death of John the Baptist","Feeding of the 5,000 → walking on water","Peter's confession → first Passion prediction → Transfiguration","Journey toward Jerusalem","Entry into Jerusalem → Temple incident → Olivet Discourse","Passion narrative"]},{"id":3,"title":"Where Matthew Departs — and Why","paragraphs":["Matthew keeps Mark's basic narrative sequence but redistributes teaching material into five large discourse blocks (chs. 5–7, 10, 13, 18, 24–25), each closed with a formula (\"when Jesus had finished these sayings…\"). The Sermon on the Mount is the clearest case: much of its content parallels material Luke places in several separate settings.","This five-discourse architecture is widely read as a deliberate echo of the five books of the Torah — Matthew presenting Jesus as the new Moses, structuring the whole Gospel to make that typology visible at the level of macro-structure."],"confidence":{"symbol":"◐","label":"majority view — the Torah-echo reading of the five discourses is widely held but not universal"},"embed":"matthew-discourse1"},{"id":4,"title":"Where Luke Departs — and Why","predictReveal":true,"paragraphs":["The larger structural feature unique to Luke is the Travel Narrative (Luke 9:51–19:27), roughly a third of the Gospel. Luke interrupts Mark's sequence where Jesus \"sets his face to go to Jerusalem\" and inserts a long, loosely organized block of material — much of it unparalleled elsewhere (\"L\" material) or shared only with Matthew (\"Q\" material) — organized not by chronology but by the journey motif itself.","Many pericopes here have no fixed order relative to their Matthean parallels; Luke and Matthew often share content but place it in entirely different narrative contexts — key evidence for a sayings source (\"Q\") each evangelist incorporated independently."],"embed":"luke-travel"},{"id":5,"title":"The Double Tradition (Q Material)","paragraphs":["Where Matthew and Luke agree closely in wording but have no Markan parallel (the \"double tradition,\" commonly attributed to a hypothetical sayings source Q — from the German \"Quelle,\" meaning \"source\" — a lost document scholars hypothesize Matthew and Luke each had independent access to and drew on to compose their accounts), the order is not stable the way triple-tradition material is. Matthew files this material into his five discourses; Luke scatters the same material through the travel narrative and elsewhere.","This is one of the strongest arguments for treating Q (if it existed) as a sayings collection without a fixed narrative sequence — unlike Markan material, which both treated as a received narrative sequence to be respected.","The discovery of the Gospel of Thomas — a genuine, standalone sayings collection with no narrative framework at all — gave the Q hypothesis independent plausibility: it shows the ancient world really did produce texts of exactly the kind Q is proposed to have been."],"confidence":{"symbol":"○","label":"contested — Q's existence itself is disputed; see §9"}},{"id":6,"title":"John: A Genuinely Different Structural Logic","paragraphs":["John shares only a handful of pericopes with the Synoptics at all — essentially the feeding of the 5,000, the walking on water, the anointing at Bethany, the triumphal entry, and the Passion narrative. Almost everything else in John is unique material, organized on different principles."],"list":["Festival structure, not itinerant chronology — the ministry is organized around Jewish festivals in Jerusalem, implying a multi-year, Jerusalem-centered ministry.","The Temple incident is moved to the very beginning (John 2:13–22) rather than Passion week — the single most famous structural transposition in the canon.","Seven \"signs\" replace the Synoptic miracle catalogues, each followed by an extended discourse interpreting its meaning.","Long theological discourses and dialogues replace the short parables that dominate the Synoptics.","The last third of the Gospel (chs. 13–21, the \"Book of Glory\") is devoted almost entirely to the Farewell Discourse, Passion, and Resurrection."],"confidence":{"symbol":"●","label":"broad consensus — the Temple-incident move is read as theological framing, not a second historical event"},"embed":"john-signs"},{"id":7,"title":"What the Reordering Signals","paragraphs":["The pattern across all four Gospels is consistent: narrative material inherited from a shared tradition (principally Mark) tends to stay in place; material an evangelist controls more freely gets moved to serve that author's argument. Four distinct theological programs emerge from the same underlying events."],"list":["Mark — urgency and the \"messianic secret\": a rapid, spare sequence building toward the cross.","Matthew — Jesus as fulfillment of Torah and the new Moses: five discourses evoking the Pentateuch (the five books of Moses).","Luke — salvation history and universal mission: the Nazareth manifesto up front (Luke 4:16-30, Jesus reading Isaiah in the synagogue), a journey structure mirroring Israel's wilderness journey.","John — high Christology (emphasizing Jesus' full divinity and pre-existence, versus a \"low\" Christology that starts from his humanity) through symbolic structure: the Temple replaced by Jesus at the outset, a sign-and-discourse rhythm building to the \"hour\" of glorification."]},{"id":8,"title":"The Scholarly Frameworks Behind This Analysis","paragraphs":["Two frameworks, in particular, remain genuinely contested — the rest of this report's method (redaction and literary criticism) is widely shared regardless of which one a scholar holds."],"list":["Two-Source Hypothesis — Mark and Q as independent sources used separately by Matthew and Luke, each also drawing on their own independent material (\"M\" and \"L\") found nowhere else; explains triple-tradition order-stability and double-tradition order-instability.","Farrer Hypothesis (Mark → Matthew → Luke, no Q) — an alternative that has Luke use Matthew directly, redistributing Matthean material rather than following its arrangement.","Redaction criticism — asking, at every point an evangelist departs from a source's order or wording, what theological point the change is making.","Narrative/literary criticism — reading each Gospel's macro-structure as deliberate authorial architecture rather than incidental sequencing."],"confidence":{"symbol":"○","label":"contested — Two-Source vs. Farrer remains an open debate; both are presented here as live positions, not settled fact"}},{"id":9,"title":"Summary","paragraphs":["The four Gospels are not four independent, randomly ordered accounts of the same events, nor a single account told four times with only cosmetic differences. They show a layered structure: a shared narrative skeleton (mostly Markan) that all three Synoptics respect, a body of teaching material each author freely reshuffles to make his own theological case, and — in John — an almost entirely independent architecture organized around festivals and signs.","The places where the order breaks are not noise; they are the clearest windows we have into each evangelist's distinct theological purpose."]}];
window.NT_GLOSSARY = {"pericope":"A self-contained unit of Gospel narrative — a healing, parable, controversy, or saying — short enough to be moved as a block.","redaction criticism":"The method of asking why an evangelist changed a source's wording or order, and what theological point that change makes.","literary criticism":"Reading a Gospel's structure, pacing, and arrangement as deliberate authorial storytelling craft — the same way a critic reads a novel's architecture — rather than as an incidental byproduct of stitching sources together.","Q":"A hypothetical lost sayings source scholars propose to explain material Matthew and Luke share but Mark lacks.","Gospel of Thomas":"A 2nd-century sayings collection (no narrative, no Passion) discovered in 1945 — often cited as proof that gospel-like texts built entirely from sayings, with no story framework, genuinely circulated in antiquity.","Two-Source Hypothesis":"The view that Matthew and Luke each independently combined Mark with a sayings source (Q), plus their own independent material (\"M\" and \"L\") found nowhere else.","Farrer Hypothesis":"The view that Luke used Matthew directly, with no separate Q source, redistributing Matthean material in his own order."};
